                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1286-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ></span>Microtech Gefell M 221</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Detachable-Capsule Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The M 221 is a high-fidelity omnidirectional condenser microphone based on the company&#8217;s MK221, half-inch nickel-diaphragm capsule design.</p><p>The same capsule is used on the Josephson c617 Set, also a high-fidelity omni condenser.</p><p>The MK221 capsule is from MTG&#8217;s measurement-microphone business. The nickel diaphragm is 2 microns thick. It is polarized at 200V by the M221 circuit.</p><p>Other half-inch capsules can be used with the M 221 head amplifier, as well.</p><p>The mic&#8217;s published frequency response is nominally flat from 20Hz&ndash;20kHz, with less than 1dB variation in the high frequencies. According to MTG, the mic&#8217;s response is flat (&plusmn;2dB) down to 3.5Hz.</p><p>The M 221 ships with two spherical acoustic equalizers. Either can be slid onto the neck of the microphone after removing the capsule. </p><p>The <b>KA 3</b> is 30mm rubber sphere; it provides a 3dB boost from 4kHz&ndash;12kHz. The <b>KA 4</b>, a 40mm sphere, provides a 3dB boost from 1kHz&ndash;12kHz.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Microtech-Gefell/M-221">Microtech Gefell M 221</a> is also known as: M221.</p><p>The mic was released in 2011.</p>
